::ENG2_U04_L07_Q01::[MLA.ENG2.W.03] Which sentence is most publication-ready for a student audience?{
~A. Pre-order compliance optimizes operational food-service outcomes.#Incorrect. This is too formal for the student audience.
=B. Completing the pre-order form helps staff prepare meals students are more likely to choose.#Correct. This sentence is clear, audience-aware, and connected to the lesson evidence.
~C. The form thing helps lunch somehow.#Incorrect. This is vague and informal.
~D. Everyone should do forms because forms are forms.#Incorrect. This does not explain the reason.
}



::ENG2_U04_L07_Q02::[MLA.ENG2.C.03] Which publication choice best supports source-based communication?{
~A. Use an unrelated image to make the page look full.#Incorrect. Unrelated images weaken the communication.
~B. Remove the evidence so the design looks cleaner.#Incorrect. The communication must remain source-based.
~C. Use only a title with no explanation.#Incorrect. The audience needs the evidence and its meaning.
=D. Pair the source finding with a labeled visual that shows the same idea.#Correct. The visual should help the audience understand the evidence.
}



::ENG2_U04_L07_Q03::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Which source detail is most relevant to a message about reducing unwanted trays?{
=A. The pilot report found fewer unwanted trays after staff used student selections.#Correct. This directly supports the message.
~B. The cafeteria walls were painted last year.#Incorrect. This does not answer the research focus.
~C. The lunch menu changes by day.#Incorrect. This may be related to lunch but not to unwanted trays.
~D. Students have different class schedules.#Incorrect. This does not directly support the claim.
}



::ENG2_U04_L07_Q04::[MLA.ENG2.V.01] In this lesson, what does "publication-ready" mean?{
~A. Long enough to fill a page.#Incorrect. Length does not make writing ready to publish.
~B. Colorful even if evidence is missing.#Incorrect. Design cannot replace evidence.
=C. Accurate, polished, organized, and clear enough to share with the intended audience.#Correct. This definition matches the lesson vocabulary.
~D. Written only for the teacher.#Incorrect. Publication-ready work fits the intended audience.
}



::ENG2_U04_L07_Q05::[MLA.ENG2.W.03] Which sentence best explains source evidence?{
=A. This finding matters because it shows how student choices can help staff prepare fewer unwanted meals.#Correct. This explains why the evidence matters.
~B. The source is a source.#Incorrect. This does not explain the evidence.
~C. Lunch forms are about lunch.#Incorrect. This is circular and vague.
~D. The report had words.#Incorrect. This does not explain meaning.
}



::ENG2_U04_L07_Q06::[MLA.ENG2.L.01] Which sentence uses standard English conventions?{
~A. Because the report found fewer trays.#Incorrect. This is a fragment.
~B. Report found trays fewer selections.#Incorrect. This is not standard English word order.
~C. The report, fewer trays, because selections.#Incorrect. This is not a complete sentence.
=D. The report found fewer unwanted trays after staff used student selections.#Correct. This is a complete, clear sentence.
}



::ENG2_U04_L07_Q07::[MLA.ENG2.C.03] Which team decision best prepares a product for publication?{
~A. Publish without checking the source.#Incorrect. Accuracy must be checked first.
=B. Review the evidence, explanation, wording, visual, and conventions before sharing.#Correct. This is a complete publication-readiness check.
~C. Let the visual contradict the evidence.#Incorrect. The visual must support the evidence.
~D. Remove all audience-focused language.#Incorrect. Publication requires audience awareness.
}



::ENG2_U04_L07_Q08::[MLA.ENG2.W.03] Which revision improves academic voice?{
~A. The form thing is cool.#Incorrect. This is informal and vague.
~B. Forms are awesome and fixed stuff.#Incorrect. This is informal and exaggerated.
=C. The pre-order form can help staff prepare meals based on student selections.#Correct. This is precise, clear, and evidence-based.
~D. Everybody better do it now.#Incorrect. This is demanding, not explanatory.
}



::ENG2_U04_L07_Q09::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Which sentence uses source information responsibly?{
~A. The pilot proves all cafeteria waste will end everywhere.#Incorrect. This overgeneralizes the source.
~B. The source should be ignored because it is about lunch.#Incorrect. The source is relevant.
~C. The source says students never need reminders.#Incorrect. This contradicts the lesson evidence.
=D. The pilot report suggests that pre-order forms reduced unwanted trays in the pilot setting.#Correct. This stays within what the source supports.
}



::ENG2_U04_L07_Q10::[MLA.ENG2.C.03] Which visual should be revised before publication?{
=A. A random image of school lockers with no connection to the evidence.#Correct. This visual does not support the source-based message.
~B. A labeled chart showing the change in unwanted trays.#Incorrect. This supports the message.
~C. A simple icon next to the action step.#Incorrect. This can support the audience if it is clear.
~D. A chart caption that explains the source finding.#Incorrect. This helps the audience understand.
}



::ENG2_U04_L07_Q11::[MLA.ENG2.W.03] Which transition best connects a benefit to a challenge?{
~A. In addition#Incorrect. This adds related information.
~B. Similarly#Incorrect. This signals similarity.
=C. However#Correct. This transition signals contrast or complication.
~D. For example#Incorrect. This introduces an example.
}



::ENG2_U04_L07_Q12::[MLA.ENG2.L.01] Which sentence should be revised for clarity?{
~A. The report found fewer unwanted trays after the pilot.#Incorrect. This is clear.
=B. Forms, charts, trays because report.#Correct. This wording is unclear and incomplete.
~C. Reminders helped more students complete the form.#Incorrect. This is clear.
~D. The visual should support the source finding.#Incorrect. This is clear.
}



::ENG2_U04_L07_Q13::[MLA.ENG2.C.03] Which final product best matches the lesson goal?{
=A. A student-facing digital flyer with accurate evidence, clear explanation, and a labeled visual.#Correct. This combines source-based writing and multimedia publication.
~B. A paragraph copied from the report with no revision.#Incorrect. The product must be adapted for the audience.
~C. A decorative poster with no source evidence.#Incorrect. The product must be source-based.
~D. A private note that never identifies an audience.#Incorrect. Publication requires audience awareness.
}



::ENG2_U04_L07_Q14::[MLA.ENG2.W.03] Which statement is too vague for publication-ready writing?{
~A. The pilot report found fewer unwanted trays.#Incorrect. This is specific.
~B. Reminders helped students complete the form.#Incorrect. This is clear.
~C. A chart can show the before-and-after change.#Incorrect. This is clear.
=D. The lunch thing got better somehow.#Correct. This is vague and informal.
}



::ENG2_U04_L07_Q15::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Which evidence pairing best supports the message?{
~A. Locker color and classroom seating.#Incorrect. These details do not support the research message.
~B. Menu font and hallway traffic.#Incorrect. These do not answer the research question.
=C. Fewer unwanted trays from the pilot report and improved form completion from reminders.#Correct. These details work together to support the message.
~D. Random student opinions with no source connection.#Incorrect. This is not reliable source-based support.
}



::ENG2_U04_L07_Q16::[MLA.ENG2.C.03] Which question should a team ask before publishing?{
~A. Can we make the design busy enough to distract from weak evidence?#Incorrect. Design should not hide weak evidence.
=B. Will the audience understand the evidence, explanation, visual, and action step?#Correct. This question checks publication readiness.
~C. Can we skip checking source accuracy?#Incorrect. Accuracy is required.
~D. Can we use a visual that is unrelated but colorful?#Incorrect. The visual should support the message.
}



::ENG2_U04_L07_Q17::[MLA.ENG2.W.03] Which sentence best combines evidence and explanation?{
~A. The report said trays.#Incorrect. This is too vague.
~B. Trays are in cafeterias.#Incorrect. This does not explain evidence.
=C. The pilot report found fewer unwanted trays, showing that student selections helped staff prepare meals more accurately.#Correct. This integrates evidence and explains its meaning.
~D. Evidence is good to have.#Incorrect. This does not explain the source finding.
}



::ENG2_U04_L07_Q18::[MLA.ENG2.V.01] Which word best describes writing that uses exact source-based language instead of vague wording?{
=A. Precision#Correct. Precision means using exact words so the message is clear.
~B. Decoration#Incorrect. Decoration is visual, not wording accuracy.
~C. Confusion#Incorrect. Confusion is the problem precise wording helps prevent.
~D. Guessing#Incorrect. Source-based communication should not rely on guessing.
}



::ENG2_U04_L07_Q19::[MLA.ENG2.L.01] Which revision fixes the fragment: "When students complete the form."{
~A. When students complete the form and.#Incorrect. This is still incomplete.
=B. When students complete the form, staff can prepare meals based on student selections.#Correct. This revision creates a complete sentence.
~C. When students.#Incorrect. This is still a fragment.
~D. Complete the when form students.#Incorrect. This is unclear.
}



::ENG2_U04_L07_Q20::[MLA.ENG2.C.03] Which action weakens publication quality?{
~A. Checking that the chart matches the source finding.#Incorrect. This strengthens publication quality.
~B. Revising wording for student readers.#Incorrect. This improves audience fit.
~C. Explaining why the evidence matters.#Incorrect. This strengthens the message.
=D. Adding a visual that contradicts the evidence.#Correct. A contradictory visual confuses the audience and weakens accuracy.
}



::ENG2_U04_L07_Q21::[MLA.ENG2.W.03] Which sentence has the strongest tone for an informational flyer?{
=A. Completing the form helps the cafeteria prepare meals students are more likely to choose.#Correct. This tone is clear, respectful, and informational.
~B. Only careless students forget the form.#Incorrect. This tone is blaming.
~C. Everybody better submit the form immediately.#Incorrect. This tone is demanding.
~D. Forms are super amazing forever.#Incorrect. This tone is exaggerated.
}



::ENG2_U04_L07_Q22::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Which note best paraphrases the source finding? Original: "Reminders helped more students complete the pre-order form after the first week."{
~A. Students never needed reminders.#Incorrect. This contradicts the original.
~B. Reminders made lunch unnecessary.#Incorrect. This changes the meaning.
=C. After the first week, reminders increased student completion of the form.#Correct. This keeps the meaning while changing the wording.
~D. Students completed no forms after reminders.#Incorrect. This contradicts the original.
}



::ENG2_U04_L07_Q23::[MLA.ENG2.C.03] Chart data: Before the lunch pre-order pilot, students left 42 unwanted trays. After the pilot, students left 18 unwanted trays. Which caption best supports this chart about unwanted trays?{
~A. A chart is shown here.#Incorrect. This does not explain what the chart means.
~B. This is colorful.#Incorrect. This focuses on appearance rather than evidence.
~C. Trays exist in cafeterias.#Incorrect. This is too general.
=D. The chart shows unwanted trays decreased from 42 before the pilot to 18 after the pilot.#Correct. This caption explains the visual and connects it to the evidence.
}



::ENG2_U04_L07_Q24::[MLA.ENG2.W.03] Which sentence overstates the evidence?{
~A. The pilot report found fewer unwanted trays.#Incorrect. This is measured and accurate.
=B. Pre-order forms will eliminate all cafeteria waste forever.#Correct. This claims more than the evidence proves.
~C. Reminders helped more students complete the form.#Incorrect. This accurately states the evidence.
~D. The chart can help students see the change.#Incorrect. This is reasonable.
}



::ENG2_U04_L07_Q25::[MLA.ENG2.C.03] Which checklist is strongest before final publication?{
=A. Evidence is accurate, explanation is clear, audience wording fits, visual supports the message, and conventions are correct.#Correct. This checklist matches publication-ready source-based communication.
~B. The page has a picture and looks full.#Incorrect. Appearance alone is not enough.
~C. The source is copied without explanation.#Incorrect. The audience needs explanation.
~D. The message uses difficult words to sound advanced.#Incorrect. Audience-aware writing values clarity.
}
